`if x is Pattern then ...` — one-arm match sugar
================================================

`if expr is Pattern then ...` is one-arm `match` sugar. It
evaluates `expr` once, tests it against the pattern, and if
the pattern matches, binds the pattern's variables for the
duration of the `then` body. The `else` branch sees no
bindings.

This is the canonical "early-narrow" idiom for `Option` and
`Result` — instead of writing a full `match` when you only
care about one variant, write `if r is Ok(v) then use(v)`.

This file pins the surface across all seven backends:

  - Basic `Some(v)` / `None` discrimination, both branches.
  - The bound variable's type is the pattern's payload type
    (int, string, struct).
  - The bound variable is visible only in the `then` arm,
    not the `else` arm or after the `if`.
  - The pattern can appear in an `if`/`else if` chain and
    one-arm patterns can be stacked.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    enum IipMaybe[T]
        IipSome(value: T)
        IipNone

    enum IipOutcome[T]
        IipOk(value: T)
        IipBad(reason: string)

    struct IipPair
        a: int
        b: int

`if x is Some(v)` — happy path
------------------------------

`x` is `Some(42)`. The pattern matches, `v` is bound to 42
in the `then` body, and the body reads `v` to produce the
result.

    iip_unwrap_or(x: IipMaybe[int], dflt: int) -> int
        if x is IipSome(v) then
            return v
        return dflt

    #test
    test_some_path_binds_payload() -> unit
        val r = iip_unwrap_or(IipSome(42), -1)
        assert(r == 42, s"Some-path should bind v=42, got $r")

`if x is Some(v)` — failure path
--------------------------------

`x` is `None`. The pattern fails, the `then` body is
skipped, the default is returned.

    #test
    test_none_path_returns_default() -> unit
        val r = iip_unwrap_or(IipNone, -1)
        assert(r == -1, s"None-path should return default, got $r")

`if x is Pattern then ... else ...`
-----------------------------------

The else-arm form. The pattern bind is visible in the
`then` arm only; the `else` arm cannot see the binding.

    iip_label(x: IipMaybe[int]) -> string =
        if x is IipSome(v) then
            "got " + str(v)
        else
            "none"

    #test
    test_else_arm() -> unit
        assert(iip_label(IipSome(7)) == "got 7",  "some 7")
        assert(iip_label(IipSome(0)) == "got 0",  "some 0")
        assert(iip_label(IipNone)    == "none",   "none")

String payload bind
-------------------

The bound payload type follows the pattern. A `Some(string)`
gives a `string` binding inside the `then` arm; concat works
on it directly.

    iip_describe_str(x: IipMaybe[string]) -> string =
        if x is IipSome(s) then
            "[" + s + "]"
        else
            "<empty>"

    #test
    test_string_payload_bind() -> unit
        assert(iip_describe_str(IipSome("hi"))  == "[hi]",   "hi")
        assert(iip_describe_str(IipSome(""))    == "[]",     "empty payload")
        assert(iip_describe_str(IipNone)        == "<empty>", "none")

Struct payload bind
-------------------

The pattern can carry a struct payload. The binding inside
the `then` arm has the struct type and supports field
access.

    iip_pair_sum(x: IipMaybe[IipPair]) -> int =
        if x is IipSome(p) then
            p.a + p.b
        else
            0

    #test
    test_struct_payload_bind() -> unit
        assert(iip_pair_sum(IipSome(IipPair(3, 4))) == 7,  "3+4")
        assert(iip_pair_sum(IipSome(IipPair(0, 0))) == 0,  "0+0")
        assert(iip_pair_sum(IipNone)                == 0,  "none")

Two-variant data enum — chained `if .. is ..`
---------------------------------------------

A two-payload-variant enum `Outcome[T]` is `Ok(value)` or
`Bad(reason)`. Two stacked `if .. is ..` patterns
distinguish the two; the order of the checks doesn't matter
because each variant is matched independently.

    iip_format_outcome(o: IipOutcome[int]) -> string
        if o is IipOk(v) then
            return "ok=" + str(v)
        if o is IipBad(why) then
            return "bad=" + why
        return "??"

    #test
    test_chained_one_arm_patterns() -> unit
        assert(iip_format_outcome(IipOk(99))        == "ok=99",       "ok")
        assert(iip_format_outcome(IipBad("oops"))   == "bad=oops",    "bad")

Pattern doesn't bind in the else arm
------------------------------------

After `if x is Some(v) then ...`, the binding `v` is not
visible in the else arm. We test this indirectly: the else
arm reads only outer-scope names and `dflt`, and produces
the expected default, which would not work if the binding
leaked through.

    iip_unwrap_or_string(x: IipMaybe[int], dflt: string) -> string =
        if x is IipSome(v) then
            "val=" + str(v)
        else
            "default=" + dflt

    #test
    test_else_arm_does_not_see_binding() -> unit
        val none_path = iip_unwrap_or_string(IipNone, "missing")
        assert(none_path == "default=missing", s"got '$none_path'")
        val some_path = iip_unwrap_or_string(IipSome(5), "missing")
        assert(some_path == "val=5", s"got '$some_path'")

Pattern check called twice on the same value
--------------------------------------------

The same value passed through two distinct `if .. is ..`
calls returns consistent results. Pins that the pattern
check is a pure read — no side-effect on the value being
matched, no buffer corruption after a first inspection.

    #test
    test_pattern_check_is_pure() -> unit
        val x = IipSome(123)
        val a = iip_unwrap_or(x, 0)
        val b = iip_unwrap_or(x, 0)
        assert(a == b, s"two checks should match: $a vs $b")
        assert(a == 123, s"got $a")
